### What is the difference between this and super?

Both keywords have three uses: accessing fields, calling methods, and calling constructors.

### How do this and super resolve field shadowing?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class Account {
    protected String name = "Account";
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private String name = "SavingsAccount"; // shadows parent field

    public void printNames() {
        System.out.println(this.name);   // "SavingsAccount" (this class)
        System.out.println(super.name);  // "Account" (parent class)
    }
}
```

### How do this and super work with method calls?

```java
public class Account {
    public void withdraw(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient funds");
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private static final java.math.BigDecimal MIN_BALANCE = new java.math.BigDecimal("1000");

    @Override
    public void withdraw(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        if (balance.subtract(amount).compareTo(MIN_BALANCE) < 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Would go below Rs." + MIN_BALANCE);
        }
        super.withdraw(amount); // call parent's withdraw: does the actual debit
        // this.withdraw(amount) would cause INFINITE RECURSION.
    }
}
```

### How do this() and super() work with constructors?

```java
public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private java.math.BigDecimal balance;
    private final String holderName;

    public Account(String accountId, String holderName, java.math.BigDecimal balance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.holderName = holderName;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public Account(String accountId, String holderName) {
        this(accountId, holderName, java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO); // this() chains to primary
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private final java.math.BigDecimal interestRate;

    public SavingsAccount(String id, String name, java.math.BigDecimal bal, java.math.BigDecimal rate) {
        super(id, name, bal); // super() calls Account's constructor
        this.interestRate = rate;
    }

    public SavingsAccount(String id, String name) {
        this(id, name, java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO, new java.math.BigDecimal("0.04")); // this() chains
    }

    // Cannot use both this() and super() in same constructor:
    // public SavingsAccount(String id) {
    //     super(id, "Unknown", BigDecimal.ZERO); // first statement
    //     this(id, "Unknown"); // compile error: second constructor call not allowed
    // }
}
```

### What happens when you write no constructor call?

Java inserts super() implicitly, calling the parent's no-arg constructor.

```java
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    public CurrentAccount() {
        // Java inserts: super(); -> calls Account's no-arg constructor
        // If Account has no no-arg constructor -> compile error
    }
}
```

### How is this used as a reference?

```java
public class Trade {
    private Account account;

    public void associateWith(Account account) {
        this.account = account;  // this.account = field. account = parameter.
        account.addTrade(this);  // this = current Trade object passed to Account
    }
}
```

### How do this and super compare?

| Aspect | this | super |
|--------|------|-------|
| Refers to | Current object instance | Parent class |
| Field access | this.balance (current class) | super.balance (parent class) |
| Method call | this.method() (current version) | super.method() (parent version) |
| Constructor | this(args) (same class) | super(args) (parent class) |
| Position | First statement in constructor | First statement in constructor |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is calling this.method() inside an overridden method, causing infinite recursion.

```java
// WRONG: infinite recursion
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    @Override public void withdraw(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        this.withdraw(amount); // calls THIS method again, infinite loop
    }
}

// CORRECT: call parent's version
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    @Override public void withdraw(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        super.withdraw(amount); // calls Account.withdraw()
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In records, the canonical constructor is called implicitly. There is no explicit super() because records extend Record.
- In Spring, constructor injection uses this pattern: the constructor receives dependencies and assigns them to fields using this.field = parameter.
- The this keyword is also used in the Builder pattern to enable fluent method chaining: return this;
